A CLASS WORTH ITS OWN LINE (coord, 2026-09-23; from this revision's own evidence):

**A MUTATION HARNESS THAT DOES NOT RESTORE IN A `finally` DOES NOT FAIL — IT POISONS THE NEXT MEASUREMENT.** The symptom appears in a different run than the cause. Measured here: an earlier version of my drill script timed out mid-mutant (the mutant hung, which is the defect's shape), died before restoring, and left the mutation in the tree — so the NEXT drill run measured an already-mutated tree, and the "missing release line" I then chased was my own unrestored mutant rather than a defect in the fix. It cost a wrong reading and a detour through the launcher's failure paths before the cause was found.

This is the same shape as the rest of the fleet's findings one level up: an instrument whose failure mode is indistinguishable from a result. A green drill proves nothing if the tree under it was already mutated, and nothing in the output says which tree was measured.

RULES THAT FOLLOW (now implemented in ~/cap-evidence/0lj3-revise-drills.py):
- restore the mutation in a `finally`, so a timeout, a hang or an exception cannot leave it behind;
- treat a HANG as a kill (a mutant that hangs is the defect's shape), which also bounds the drill;
- re-assert green after restoring, so "the mutant was killed" and "the tree is back" are both observed rather than assumed;
- verify the tree after the run (grep for the fixed form) before drawing any conclusion from a later run.

description:
The cgei fix (d0f01545) replaces "absent and zero are indistinguishable" with its mirror image: "absent and uniform are indistinguishable". The check now false-REDS on a legitimate layout.

OBSERVED (DRIVEN by the independent review of d0f01545):
Control: both elements present, both style="padding:14px" - left insets equal, so the property the check asserts genuinely HOLDS and it should PASS.
  - PRE-fix driver: PASS, 46/0, exit 0.
  - NEW driver:    FAIL, 45/1, exit 1 -> {"bodyPadding":"14px","headPadding":"14px","bodyLeft":null,"headLeft":null}
The failure detail contradicts its own verdict: it shows both padding strings and still reports failure, because only the TOKEN COUNT differed.

MECHANISM: padLeft splits the padding string on whitespace and takes index [1]. A UNIFORM single-value shorthand has no [1], so it returns null, and null now fails - even though both elements are present and their padding was read.

FIX SHAPE (suggested by the reviewer - keeps absence failing, accepts a uniform shorthand):
  const padLeft = (value) => {
    const t = String(value ?? "").trim().split(/\s+/).filter(Boolean);
    if (!t.length) return null;                 // element absent -> FAIL, as intended
    const part = t.length >= 2 ? t[1] : t[0];   // 1 value == all four sides
    const n = parseFloat(part);
    return Number.isFinite(n) ? n : null;
  };

ACCEPTANCE: (1) the control above PASSES - both elements present, both padding:14px, 46/0 exit 0; (2) a genuinely absent element still FAILS (the cgei mutant: rename #jobs-board-host and the jobs-section .panel-head away -> RED, exit 1); (3) both recorded, and the check shown passing when the defect is present so it is not itself vacuous.

SEVERITY, as graded by the reviewer and not to be inflated: NOT a blocker. It is fail-CLOSED (red on a good state) rather than fail-open, it is unreachable today because the shipped tree uses 12px 14px for both, and the driver is registered as "named" so no gate runs it. But it is a check that can fail for the WRONG REASON, which is the exact class this commit set out to remove - so it is worth fixing promptly, with step (1) as its regression test.

EVIDENCE: ~/cap-evidence/rv-d0f01545/ (reviewer transcripts + the mutants' sed commands).

